---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const title = "Pikos vs Notion, Obsidian, NotePlan, and Others";
const description = "How Pikos compares to Notion, Obsidian, Logseq, NotePlan, TickTick, Things, Todoist, and the Apple apps. Honest feature comparison.";

const apps = [
  { name: "Pikos",    notes: true,  tasks: true,  calendar: true,  localFirst: true,  noAccount: true,  free: true  },
  { name: "NotePlan", notes: true,  tasks: true,  calendar: true,  localFirst: true,  noAccount: false, free: false },
  { name: "Notion",   notes: true,  tasks: true,  calendar: true,  localFirst: false, noAccount: false, free: true  },
  { name: "Obsidian", notes: true,  tasks: false, calendar: false, localFirst: true,  noAccount: true,  free: true  },
  { name: "Logseq",   notes: true,  tasks: true,  calendar: false, localFirst: true,  noAccount: true,  free: true  },
  { name: "TickTick", notes: false, tasks: true,  calendar: true,  localFirst: false, noAccount: false, free: false },
  { name: "Things",   notes: false, tasks: true,  calendar: false, localFirst: true,  noAccount: true,  free: false },
  { name: "Todoist",  notes: false, tasks: true,  calendar: false, localFirst: false, noAccount: false, free: false },
];

const columns = [
  { key: "notes",      label: "Notes" },
  { key: "tasks",      label: "Tasks" },
  { key: "calendar",   label: "Calendar" },
  { key: "localFirst", label: "On device" },
  { key: "noAccount",  label: "No account" },
  { key: "free",       label: "Free" },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-8 py-20">

    <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-neutral-400 transition-colors hover:text-primary-500 dark:text-neutral-500 dark:hover:text-primary-400">
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m15 18-6-6 6-6"/></svg>
      Home
    </a>

    <h1 class="mt-8 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">How Pikos compares</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-500 dark:text-neutral-400">
      Most productivity apps do one thing. Notes, or tasks, or calendar. Pikos does all three in one window, stored on your device, no account required.
    </p>

    <!-- Comparison table -->
    <div class="mt-12 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-700">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-neutral-200 bg-neutral-50 dark:border-neutral-700 dark:bg-neutral-900">
            <th class="w-[100px] px-3 py-2.5 text-left text-xs font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">App</th>
            {columns.map((col) => (
              <th class="px-2 py-2.5 text-center text-xs font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">{col.label}</th>
            ))}
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {apps.map((app, i) => (
            <tr class:list={[
              "border-b border-neutral-100 last:border-b-0 dark:border-neutral-800",
              i === 0 && "bg-warning-400/10 dark:bg-warning-500/15",
            ]}>
              <td class:list={[
                "px-3 py-2.5 text-left font-medium",
                i === 0 ? "text-warning-500 dark:text-warning-400" : "text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50",
              ]}>
                {app.name}
              </td>
              {columns.map((col) => {
                const val = app[col.key as keyof typeof app];
                return (
                  <td class="px-2 py-2.5 text-center">
                    {val === true ? (
                      <svg class="mx-auto h-4 w-4 text-emerald-500" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5"/></svg>
                    ) : (
                      <span class="text-neutral-300 dark:text-neutral-600">—</span>
                    )}
                  </td>
                );
              })}
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-neutral-400 dark:text-neutral-500">
      Checked September 2026. These apps all move, so if something here has gone out of date, <a href="/feedback" class="hover:text-primary-500 hover:underline dark:hover:text-primary-400">tell me</a> and I'll correct it.
    </p>

    <!-- Prose comparison sections -->
    <div class="mt-16 space-y-10 text-base leading-relaxed text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-300">

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs Notes, Reminders and Calendar</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          You already have all three, they cost nothing, and they sync across your devices without you setting anything up. That's a real advantage and Pikos doesn't match it yet. What you don't get is any connection between them. The note about a meeting sits in one app, the task it produced in another, the meeting itself in a third, and nothing ties them together. In Pikos a page is all three at once, so the note, the task and the time are one thing rather than three you keep in step by hand.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs Obsidian</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Obsidian is great for linking notes and building a personal knowledge base. It doesn't have tasks or a calendar. You need plugins for both, and they don't talk to each other well. If your workflow is mostly writing and linking, Obsidian is the right tool. If you also need to track and schedule things without switching apps, Pikos does that natively.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs Notion</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Notion covers all three too, and with far more power than Pikos: databases, views, templates, and a whole layer for working with other people. The difference is where the work lives. Notion wants an account from the first minute and keeps your pages on its servers, and its offline mode is a cache of what you had open rather than the real thing. Pikos keeps everything in a file on your disk and behaves the same with the network off. If you're building something with a team, Notion is the stronger tool. If you want your own notes to stay yours, that's the entire point of Pikos.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs NotePlan</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          NotePlan is the closest alternative. Notes, tasks, and calendar in one app. It requires an Apple account and a subscription. Pikos is a free download with no account required, and your data stays on your device in a standard format. The Mac App Store and iPhone versions will be one-time purchases when they ship. NotePlan has iOS support that Pikos doesn't have yet. If you need cross-device sync today, NotePlan is more complete. If you'd rather own your data and pay once instead of monthly, Pikos is the better fit.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs TickTick</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          TickTick is a polished cloud-based task manager with a calendar. Its note-taking is basic. Text attached to tasks, not a real editor. Pikos keeps everything on your device and comes with a full rich text editor, so you can write real documents next to your tasks. If you want cross-platform sync and habit tracking, TickTick does that. If you want your data on your device and real notes alongside tasks, Pikos is a better choice.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Pikos vs Things</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Things is beautifully designed and keeps everything on your Mac, but it only does tasks. No notes, no calendar. Pikos adds both. Things costs $50; the Pikos desktop app is a free download. If you want a focused, minimal task manager and nothing else, Things is hard to beat. If you want tasks integrated with notes and a calendar in one window, Pikos does more.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-50">Your data, your format</h2>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Pikos imports CSV and Markdown files, so switching from another app is straightforward. Your data lives in a standard file on your drive. You can back it up, copy it, or export it anytime. No proprietary format, no lock-in. If you ever stop using Pikos, your data is already in a format anything else can read.
        </p>
      </section>

    </div>

  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>

  </style>
